1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,787
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,967 --> 00:00:04,796
The American middle class works harder than

3
00:00:04,796 --> 00:00:05,717
almost anyone.

4
00:00:05,897 --> 00:00:08,113
Two incomes, long hours,

5
00:00:08,113 --> 00:00:09,682
kids, a mortgage.

6
00:00:09,862 --> 00:00:12,092
And yet, so many middle class families

7
00:00:12,092 --> 00:00:14,146
feel like they're running in place.

8
00:00:14,326 --> 00:00:16,573
They earn more than their parents did,

9
00:00:16,573 --> 00:00:18,761
and somehow have less breathing room.

10
00:00:18,941 --> 00:00:21,174
Part of that is rising costs,

11
00:00:21,174 --> 00:00:21,867
no doubt.

12
00:00:22,047 --> 00:00:23,334
But part of it is where the

13
00:00:23,334 --> 00:00:23,858
money goes.

14
00:00:24,038 --> 00:00:27,142
Today, twenty things the middle class wastes

15
00:00:27,142 --> 00:00:29,188
money on, and what to do with

16
00:00:29,188 --> 00:00:30,528
that money instead.

17
00:00:30,879 --> 00:00:32,655
Here's the squeeze in one number.

18
00:00:32,835 --> 00:00:35,176
According to the Pew Research Center,

19
00:00:35,176 --> 00:00:37,580
the share of American adults living in

20
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:40,364
middle income households fell from sixty one

21
00:00:40,364 --> 00:00:42,895
percent in nineteen seventy one to about

22
00:00:42,895 --> 00:00:45,490
fifty one percent in twenty twenty three.

23
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:48,262
Pew's research shows the middle class has

24
00:00:48,262 --> 00:00:49,858
been shrinking for decades,

25
00:00:49,858 --> 00:00:51,335
while costs like housing,

26
00:00:51,335 --> 00:00:53,876
health care and college have climbed faster

27
00:00:53,876 --> 00:00:55,058
than many paychecks.

28
00:00:55,238 --> 00:00:57,490
That's real, and it's not your fault.

29
00:00:57,670 --> 00:00:59,098
But here's the hopeful part.

30
00:00:59,278 --> 00:01:01,400
Inside a typical middle class budget,

31
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,808
there's often a surprising amount of money

32
00:01:03,808 --> 00:01:06,102
leaking into things that don't make life

33
00:01:06,102 --> 00:01:06,790
much better.

34
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:08,090
Find those leaks,

35
00:01:08,090 --> 00:01:10,198
and you can create room to save,

36
00:01:10,198 --> 00:01:11,449
invest and breathe,

37
00:01:11,449 --> 00:01:13,228
even in an expensive world.

38
00:01:13,578 --> 00:01:15,801
We'll go through these twenty from three

39
00:01:15,801 --> 00:01:16,190
angles.

40
00:01:16,370 --> 00:01:17,775
First, the numbers.

41
00:01:17,955 --> 00:01:20,013
The big ticket items where the math

42
00:01:20,013 --> 00:01:20,602
is brutal.

43
00:01:20,782 --> 00:01:22,860
Second, the psychology.

44
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:25,662
The social pressure and emotions that push

45
00:01:25,662 --> 00:01:27,846
middle class families to overspend.

46
00:01:28,026 --> 00:01:29,663
And third, the playbook.

47
00:01:29,843 --> 00:01:31,816
The quiet leaks in the system,

48
00:01:31,816 --> 00:01:33,393
from taxes to insurance,

49
00:01:33,393 --> 00:01:35,300
and exactly how to plug them.

50
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:36,061
By the end,

51
00:01:36,061 --> 00:01:37,909
you'll probably find at least a few

52
00:01:37,909 --> 00:01:39,969
hundred dollars a month you didn't know

53
00:01:39,969 --> 00:01:40,391
you had.

54
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:42,495
Let's start with the numbers,

55
00:01:42,495 --> 00:01:44,491
and the biggest one is sitting in

56
00:01:44,491 --> 00:01:46,487
almost every driveway in America.

57
00:01:46,838 --> 00:01:47,708
Our sources.

58
00:01:47,888 --> 00:01:49,857
Middle class data comes from the Pew

59
00:01:49,857 --> 00:01:50,733
Research Center.

60
00:01:50,913 --> 00:01:53,330
Car prices and loans come from Kelley

61
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:54,767
Blue Book and Edmunds.

62
00:01:54,947 --> 00:01:57,270
Wedding costs come from The Knot's Real

63
00:01:57,270 --> 00:01:58,163
Weddings Study.

64
00:01:58,343 --> 00:02:00,859
Food waste comes from the US Department

65
00:02:00,859 --> 00:02:01,826
of Agriculture.

66
00:02:02,006 --> 00:02:04,720
Home renovation returns come from the Cost

67
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:06,012
versus Value report.

68
00:02:06,192 --> 00:02:08,873
Sports betting data comes from the American

69
00:02:08,873 --> 00:02:10,058
Gaming Association.

70
00:02:10,238 --> 00:02:13,126
Growth examples use a stated seven percent

71
00:02:13,126 --> 00:02:14,638
average yearly return,

72
00:02:14,638 --> 00:02:16,357
which is an illustration,

73
00:02:16,357 --> 00:02:17,320
not a promise.

74
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:18,829
This is education,

75
00:02:18,829 --> 00:02:20,379
not financial advice.

76
00:02:20,730 --> 00:02:21,589
Angle one.

77
00:02:21,769 --> 00:02:22,593
The numbers.

78
00:02:22,773 --> 00:02:25,766
Seven big expenses where a single decision

79
00:02:25,766 --> 00:02:28,189
can cost or save tens of thousands

80
00:02:28,189 --> 00:02:28,973
of dollars.

81
00:02:29,323 --> 00:02:30,530
Waste number one.

82
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:32,405
New cars on long loans.

83
00:02:32,755 --> 00:02:35,089
Kelley Blue Book reported that the average

84
00:02:35,089 --> 00:02:37,034
price paid for a new vehicle passed

85
00:02:37,034 --> 00:02:39,535
fifty thousand dollars in twenty twenty five.

86
00:02:39,715 --> 00:02:41,442
To make that affordable,

87
00:02:41,442 --> 00:02:43,960
many buyers stretch loans to six or

88
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:44,823
seven years.

89
00:02:45,003 --> 00:02:47,609
Edmunds has reported average new car payments

90
00:02:47,609 --> 00:02:49,694
above seven hundred dollars a month.

91
00:02:49,874 --> 00:02:51,258
For a two car household,

92
00:02:51,258 --> 00:02:53,219
that can mean well over a thousand

93
00:02:53,219 --> 00:02:55,411
dollars a month going to vehicles that

94
00:02:55,411 --> 00:02:57,083
lose value every single year.

95
00:02:57,434 --> 00:02:59,721
The real cost isn't just the payment.

96
00:02:59,901 --> 00:03:03,171
It's depreciation, higher insurance on newer cars,

97
00:03:03,171 --> 00:03:05,265
and the interest on a long loan.

98
00:03:05,445 --> 00:03:08,044
And because long loans often leave you

99
00:03:08,044 --> 00:03:10,301
owing more than the car is worth,

100
00:03:10,301 --> 00:03:13,105
many people roll negative equity into the

101
00:03:13,105 --> 00:03:15,499
next loan, starting the cycle over.

102
00:03:15,679 --> 00:03:17,863
It's one of the biggest reasons middle

103
00:03:17,863 --> 00:03:19,818
class households struggle to save,

104
00:03:19,818 --> 00:03:21,658
and one of the least questioned.

105
00:03:22,008 --> 00:03:22,913
The fix.

106
00:03:23,093 --> 00:03:24,871
Buy reliable used cars,

107
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:26,727
ideally a few years old,

108
00:03:26,727 --> 00:03:29,046
and pay as much as possible in

109
00:03:29,046 --> 00:03:29,432
cash.

110
00:03:29,612 --> 00:03:31,145
Keep them for many years.

111
00:03:31,325 --> 00:03:32,652
When a car loan ends,

112
00:03:32,652 --> 00:03:34,864
keep making the same payment into a

113
00:03:34,864 --> 00:03:37,014
savings account for your next car.

114
00:03:37,194 --> 00:03:38,203
Over a lifetime,

115
00:03:38,203 --> 00:03:40,474
this one habit can be worth hundreds

116
00:03:40,474 --> 00:03:41,989
of thousands of dollars.

117
00:03:42,339 --> 00:03:43,511
Waste number two.

118
00:03:43,691 --> 00:03:45,003
More house than they need.

119
00:03:45,353 --> 00:03:47,713
Lenders often approve buyers for much more

120
00:03:47,713 --> 00:03:49,567
than they can comfortably afford.

121
00:03:49,747 --> 00:03:51,821
And a bigger house costs more in

122
00:03:51,821 --> 00:03:52,859
every direction.

123
00:03:53,039 --> 00:03:54,394
A bigger mortgage,

124
00:03:54,394 --> 00:03:56,051
higher property taxes,

125
00:03:56,051 --> 00:03:58,686
higher insurance, higher utilities,

126
00:03:58,686 --> 00:04:01,096
more furniture and more repairs.

127
00:04:01,276 --> 00:04:03,158
A common rule of thumb is to

128
00:04:03,158 --> 00:04:05,511
budget around one to two percent of

129
00:04:05,511 --> 00:04:08,268
a home's value every year for maintenance

130
00:04:08,268 --> 00:04:08,671
alone.

131
00:04:09,022 --> 00:04:11,219
When housing takes too large a share

132
00:04:11,219 --> 00:04:13,538
of income, families become house poor.

133
00:04:13,718 --> 00:04:15,912
Beautiful home, no savings.

134
00:04:16,092 --> 00:04:18,820
The Department of Housing and Urban Development

135
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:21,491
considers spending more than thirty percent of

136
00:04:21,491 --> 00:04:23,058
income on housing a burden.

137
00:04:23,238 --> 00:04:25,756
Many middle class families go well beyond

138
00:04:25,756 --> 00:04:27,967
that in pursuit of the extra bedroom

139
00:04:27,967 --> 00:04:29,380
or the better zip code.

140
00:04:29,730 --> 00:04:30,670
The fix.

141
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:33,111
Decide your budget before you talk to

142
00:04:33,111 --> 00:04:34,333
a lender, not after.

143
00:04:34,513 --> 00:04:37,127
Keep total housing costs around or below

144
00:04:37,127 --> 00:04:39,546
thirty percent of gross income if you

145
00:04:39,546 --> 00:04:39,807
can.

146
00:04:39,987 --> 00:04:42,094
Remember, the house is a place to

147
00:04:42,094 --> 00:04:43,563
live, not a scoreboard.

148
00:04:43,743 --> 00:04:45,833
The smaller house with savings in the

149
00:04:45,833 --> 00:04:47,528
bank is often the richer life.

150
00:04:47,878 --> 00:04:49,143
Waste number three.

151
00:04:49,323 --> 00:04:50,542
Credit card interest.

152
00:04:50,892 --> 00:04:53,506
The Federal Reserve reports that the average

153
00:04:53,506 --> 00:04:55,764
rate on credit card accounts that paid

154
00:04:55,764 --> 00:04:58,200
interest has been above twenty percent in

155
00:04:58,200 --> 00:04:58,972
recent years.

156
00:04:59,152 --> 00:05:01,369
The Federal Reserve Bank of New York

157
00:05:01,369 --> 00:05:04,017
reported that total credit card balances in

158
00:05:04,017 --> 00:05:06,234
America passed one trillion dollars.

159
00:05:06,414 --> 00:05:08,105
A lot of that is middle class

160
00:05:08,105 --> 00:05:10,787
families covering the gap between their income

161
00:05:10,787 --> 00:05:11,662
and their life.

162
00:05:12,012 --> 00:05:13,567
Interest is pure waste.

163
00:05:13,747 --> 00:05:14,827
You get nothing for it.

164
00:05:15,007 --> 00:05:16,998
On a ten thousand dollar balance at

165
00:05:16,998 --> 00:05:18,079
twenty two percent,

166
00:05:18,079 --> 00:05:20,695
that's around two thousand two hundred dollars

167
00:05:20,695 --> 00:05:21,776
a year in interest.

168
00:05:21,956 --> 00:05:23,176
That's a vacation,

169
00:05:23,176 --> 00:05:24,397
a car repair fund,

170
00:05:24,397 --> 00:05:27,177
or a year of retirement contributions for

171
00:05:27,177 --> 00:05:29,212
many people, handed to a bank.

172
00:05:29,562 --> 00:05:30,386
The fix.

173
00:05:30,566 --> 00:05:32,307
Stop adding to the balance.

174
00:05:32,487 --> 00:05:34,195
Make a list of every card and

175
00:05:34,195 --> 00:05:36,551
attack the highest rate first with every

176
00:05:36,551 --> 00:05:37,317
extra dollar.

177
00:05:37,497 --> 00:05:39,519
Call your issuer and ask for a

178
00:05:39,519 --> 00:05:40,260
lower rate.

179
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:41,825
And once it's paid off,

180
00:05:41,825 --> 00:05:44,173
use cards only for purchases you'll pay

181
00:05:44,173 --> 00:05:45,317
in full that month.

182
00:05:45,667 --> 00:05:46,804
Waste number four.

183
00:05:46,984 --> 00:05:48,703
The very expensive wedding.

184
00:05:49,053 --> 00:05:51,505
According to The Knot's Real Weddings Study,

185
00:05:51,505 --> 00:05:53,289
the average cost of a wedding in

186
00:05:53,289 --> 00:05:55,574
the United States was around thirty three

187
00:05:55,574 --> 00:05:57,748
thousand dollars in twenty twenty four.

188
00:05:57,928 --> 00:05:58,952
For many couples,

189
00:05:58,952 --> 00:06:01,000
that's more than a down payment on

190
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:02,867
a starter home in many parts of

191
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:04,794
the country, or a big head start

192
00:06:04,794 --> 00:06:05,637
on retirement.

193
00:06:05,988 --> 00:06:07,472
Some couples pay cash,

194
00:06:07,472 --> 00:06:09,902
and if that's what they truly value,

195
00:06:09,902 --> 00:06:10,306
great.

196
00:06:10,486 --> 00:06:11,518
But many borrow,

197
00:06:11,518 --> 00:06:13,905
or drain savings they worked years to

198
00:06:13,905 --> 00:06:16,291
build, and start married life behind.

199
00:06:16,471 --> 00:06:19,782
Interestingly, a widely cited study by economists

200
00:06:19,782 --> 00:06:22,822
at Emory University found that higher wedding

201
00:06:22,822 --> 00:06:26,201
spending was not associated with longer marriages.

202
00:06:26,381 --> 00:06:27,809
The party is one day.

203
00:06:27,989 --> 00:06:30,055
The bills can last much longer.

204
00:06:30,406 --> 00:06:31,334
The fix.

205
00:06:31,514 --> 00:06:34,024
Decide what matters most to both of

206
00:06:34,024 --> 00:06:35,602
you, maybe the people,

207
00:06:35,602 --> 00:06:37,252
the food or the photos,

208
00:06:37,252 --> 00:06:38,399
and spend there.

209
00:06:38,579 --> 00:06:39,520
Cut the rest.

210
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:41,807
Consider smaller guest lists,

211
00:06:41,807 --> 00:06:42,896
off peak dates,

212
00:06:42,896 --> 00:06:44,204
or simpler venues.

213
00:06:44,384 --> 00:06:45,553
And whatever you do,

214
00:06:45,553 --> 00:06:47,716
avoid starting your marriage in debt.

215
00:06:48,067 --> 00:06:49,158
Waste number five.

216
00:06:49,338 --> 00:06:50,940
High investment fees.

217
00:06:51,290 --> 00:06:53,851
Many middle class investors pay fees they

218
00:06:53,851 --> 00:06:55,225
don't even know about.

219
00:06:55,405 --> 00:06:57,090
Fund expense ratios,

220
00:06:57,090 --> 00:06:59,364
account fees, advisor fees.

221
00:06:59,544 --> 00:07:01,718
A one percent yearly fee sounds tiny,

222
00:07:01,718 --> 00:07:03,951
but on a growing retirement account it

223
00:07:03,951 --> 00:07:05,772
can add up to tens of thousands

224
00:07:05,772 --> 00:07:07,711
of dollars or more over a working

225
00:07:07,711 --> 00:07:09,943
lifetime, because you lose the fee and

226
00:07:09,943 --> 00:07:12,176
all the growth it would have produced.

227
00:07:12,526 --> 00:07:15,690
Low cost index funds became popular exactly

228
00:07:15,690 --> 00:07:16,868
because of this.

229
00:07:17,048 --> 00:07:19,654
Many broad market index funds charge a

230
00:07:19,654 --> 00:07:22,191
small fraction of a percent per year.

231
00:07:22,371 --> 00:07:24,115
Paying for good advice can be worth

232
00:07:24,115 --> 00:07:26,009
it, but you should always know exactly

233
00:07:26,009 --> 00:07:28,153
what you're paying and what you're getting.

234
00:07:28,503 --> 00:07:29,327
The fix.

235
00:07:29,507 --> 00:07:32,029
Look up the expense ratio of every

236
00:07:32,029 --> 00:07:32,994
fund you own,

237
00:07:32,994 --> 00:07:34,849
in your four oh one k and

238
00:07:34,849 --> 00:07:35,591
elsewhere.

239
00:07:35,771 --> 00:07:37,942
Convert the fees into dollars per year.

240
00:07:38,122 --> 00:07:39,624
If you use an advisor,

241
00:07:39,624 --> 00:07:41,605
ask exactly how they're paid.

242
00:07:41,785 --> 00:07:44,109
Consider a fee only fiduciary if you

243
00:07:44,109 --> 00:07:44,884
want advice.

244
00:07:45,235 --> 00:07:46,593
Waste number six.

245
00:07:46,773 --> 00:07:48,747
Food that ends up in the trash.

246
00:07:49,097 --> 00:07:52,275
The US Department of Agriculture estimates that

247
00:07:52,275 --> 00:07:54,912
thirty to forty percent of the American

248
00:07:54,912 --> 00:07:56,399
food supply is wasted.

249
00:07:56,579 --> 00:07:58,507
A lot of that happens at home.

250
00:07:58,687 --> 00:08:00,120
Produce that goes bad,

251
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:02,596
leftovers forgotten in the back of the

252
00:08:02,596 --> 00:08:04,876
fridge, and too much food bought on

253
00:08:04,876 --> 00:08:06,245
impulse at the store.

254
00:08:06,595 --> 00:08:08,017
For a typical family,

255
00:08:08,017 --> 00:08:10,658
wasted food can easily mean hundreds of

256
00:08:10,658 --> 00:08:12,825
dollars or more a year thrown in

257
00:08:12,825 --> 00:08:13,503
the trash.

258
00:08:13,683 --> 00:08:16,339
It's money spent and then literally thrown

259
00:08:16,339 --> 00:08:16,655
away.

260
00:08:16,835 --> 00:08:18,497
And it often goes hand in hand

261
00:08:18,497 --> 00:08:21,433
with ordering takeout because there's nothing planned

262
00:08:21,433 --> 00:08:23,151
at home, so families pay twice.

263
00:08:23,501 --> 00:08:24,406
The fix.

264
00:08:24,586 --> 00:08:25,994
Plan meals for the week,

265
00:08:25,994 --> 00:08:26,991
shop with a list,

266
00:08:26,991 --> 00:08:29,160
and check the fridge before you shop.

267
00:08:29,340 --> 00:08:31,558
Make one night a week leftovers night.

268
00:08:31,738 --> 00:08:33,561
Freeze what you won't eat in time.

269
00:08:33,741 --> 00:08:36,036
These small habits can cut both food

270
00:08:36,036 --> 00:08:37,758
waste and takeout spending.

271
00:08:38,108 --> 00:08:39,269
Waste number seven.

272
00:08:39,449 --> 00:08:41,457
Gym memberships that go unused.

273
00:08:41,807 --> 00:08:43,930
Gyms know that many members sign up

274
00:08:43,930 --> 00:08:46,114
with good intentions and stop going.

275
00:08:46,294 --> 00:08:49,891
Economists Stefano DellaVigna and Ulrike Malmendier studied

276
00:08:49,891 --> 00:08:52,086
gym members and found that people on

277
00:08:52,086 --> 00:08:54,646
monthly contracts paid much more per visit

278
00:08:54,646 --> 00:08:56,597
than they would have paid with a

279
00:08:56,597 --> 00:08:57,877
pay per visit option,

280
00:08:57,877 --> 00:09:00,072
because they went far less than they

281
00:09:00,072 --> 00:09:00,621
expected.

282
00:09:00,971 --> 00:09:03,109
It's a perfect example of paying for

283
00:09:03,109 --> 00:09:04,831
the person we hope to become,

284
00:09:04,831 --> 00:09:06,613
rather than the person we are.

285
00:09:06,793 --> 00:09:09,170
The same pattern shows up with online

286
00:09:09,170 --> 00:09:11,611
courses, hobby gear and subscriptions.

287
00:09:11,791 --> 00:09:13,660
The intention feels good.

288
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:15,605
The monthly charge keeps coming.

289
00:09:15,955 --> 00:09:17,116
The fix.

290
00:09:17,296 --> 00:09:19,262
Look at how many times you actually

291
00:09:19,262 --> 00:09:20,779
went to the gym in the last

292
00:09:20,779 --> 00:09:21,510
three months.

293
00:09:21,690 --> 00:09:23,629
Divide the cost by the visits.

294
00:09:23,809 --> 00:09:25,734
If it's more than a day pass

295
00:09:25,734 --> 00:09:28,139
would cost, switch plans or cancel.

296
00:09:28,319 --> 00:09:31,117
Walking, running and home workouts are free.

297
00:09:31,468 --> 00:09:33,800
Fixing just these first seven can free

298
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:34,904
up a lot of money.

299
00:09:35,084 --> 00:09:37,274
If it's four hundred dollars a month,

300
00:09:37,274 --> 00:09:39,759
invested at a seven percent average yearly

301
00:09:39,759 --> 00:09:42,422
return, after twenty years that's roughly two

302
00:09:42,422 --> 00:09:44,256
hundred eight thousand dollars.

303
00:09:44,606 --> 00:09:46,266
Let's zoom out for a moment.

304
00:09:46,446 --> 00:09:49,393
According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics

305
00:09:49,393 --> 00:09:51,313
Consumer Expenditure Survey,

306
00:09:51,313 --> 00:09:53,712
housing is the biggest share of the

307
00:09:53,712 --> 00:09:56,043
typical American household budget,

308
00:09:56,043 --> 00:09:57,893
followed by transportation,

309
00:09:57,893 --> 00:10:00,773
then food, then health care and insurance.

310
00:10:00,953 --> 00:10:03,742
Those big categories are exactly where most

311
00:10:03,742 --> 00:10:05,817
of the middle class waste lives.

312
00:10:05,997 --> 00:10:06,879
Not in coffee.

313
00:10:07,059 --> 00:10:09,381
In houses, cars and food.

314
00:10:09,732 --> 00:10:11,218
That's actually good news.

315
00:10:11,398 --> 00:10:13,072
It means you don't need to track

316
00:10:13,072 --> 00:10:15,008
every penny to make a big difference.

317
00:10:15,188 --> 00:10:17,407
A smarter decision on your next car

318
00:10:17,407 --> 00:10:19,309
or your next home can be worth

319
00:10:19,309 --> 00:10:21,527
more than years of skipping lattes.

320
00:10:21,707 --> 00:10:24,056
Focus your energy on the big three,

321
00:10:24,056 --> 00:10:26,607
and the small stuff matters much less.

322
00:10:26,957 --> 00:10:29,380
It also means the biggest wins come

323
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:30,765
at specific moments.

324
00:10:30,945 --> 00:10:32,326
When you buy a car.

325
00:10:32,506 --> 00:10:34,179
When you sign a lease or a

326
00:10:34,179 --> 00:10:34,759
mortgage.

327
00:10:34,939 --> 00:10:35,972
When you plan a wedding.

328
00:10:36,152 --> 00:10:38,230
When you choose your retirement funds.

329
00:10:38,410 --> 00:10:40,930
Those moments only come every few years,

330
00:10:40,930 --> 00:10:43,325
so it's worth slowing down and getting

331
00:10:43,325 --> 00:10:44,018
them right.

332
00:10:44,198 --> 00:10:46,508
One good decision at the right moment

333
00:10:46,508 --> 00:10:47,820
can echo for decades.

334
00:10:48,170 --> 00:10:49,122
That's angle one.

335
00:10:49,302 --> 00:10:51,557
Cars, houses, interest,

336
00:10:51,557 --> 00:10:56,558
weddings, fees, wasted food and unused memberships.

337
00:10:56,738 --> 00:10:57,831
Now angle two,

338
00:10:57,831 --> 00:10:59,002
the psychology.

339
00:10:59,182 --> 00:11:01,953
Because middle class waste is often driven

340
00:11:01,953 --> 00:11:04,197
less by math and more by pressure.

341
00:11:04,548 --> 00:11:06,771
The middle class lives in a constant

342
00:11:06,771 --> 00:11:07,450
comparison.

343
00:11:07,630 --> 00:11:10,460
Neighbors, coworkers, parents at school,

344
00:11:10,460 --> 00:11:12,158
friends on social media.

345
00:11:12,338 --> 00:11:14,184
Everyone is quietly measuring.

346
00:11:14,364 --> 00:11:16,860
And because middle class families are close

347
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:18,950
enough to see the upper middle class

348
00:11:18,950 --> 00:11:21,214
lifestyle, the pressure to reach for it

349
00:11:21,214 --> 00:11:21,794
is strong.

350
00:11:21,974 --> 00:11:23,991
That's where the next seven wastes come

351
00:11:23,991 --> 00:11:24,250
from.

352
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:25,749
Waste number eight.

353
00:11:25,929 --> 00:11:27,287
Keeping up with the neighbors.

354
00:11:27,638 --> 00:11:30,553
Economists have studied whether people spend more

355
00:11:30,553 --> 00:11:32,398
when the people around them do.

356
00:11:32,578 --> 00:11:35,141
One study by researchers at the Federal

357
00:11:35,141 --> 00:11:38,034
Reserve Bank of Philadelphia found that when

358
00:11:38,034 --> 00:11:39,809
a neighbor won the lottery,

359
00:11:39,809 --> 00:11:42,504
nearby households were more likely to buy

360
00:11:42,504 --> 00:11:44,082
visible items like cars,

361
00:11:44,082 --> 00:11:46,646
and later more likely to face financial

362
00:11:46,646 --> 00:11:47,171
trouble.

363
00:11:47,351 --> 00:11:49,383
Seeing others spend makes us spend.

364
00:11:49,733 --> 00:11:51,102
The new SUV next door,

365
00:11:51,102 --> 00:11:53,343
the kitchen remodel down the street,

366
00:11:53,343 --> 00:11:54,899
the pool in the backyard.

367
00:11:55,079 --> 00:11:57,784
Each one quietly resets what feels normal.

368
00:11:57,964 --> 00:12:00,421
And because these purchases are visible,

369
00:12:00,421 --> 00:12:01,772
they feel like status.

370
00:12:01,952 --> 00:12:04,748
But status bought with debt is borrowed

371
00:12:04,748 --> 00:12:05,249
status.

372
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:06,552
The fix.

373
00:12:06,732 --> 00:12:09,043
Remember that you only see your neighbors'

374
00:12:09,043 --> 00:12:10,969
purchases, not their bank balances.

375
00:12:11,149 --> 00:12:12,960
Many of them may be struggling.

376
00:12:13,140 --> 00:12:14,319
Set your own goals,

377
00:12:14,319 --> 00:12:15,311
write them down,

378
00:12:15,311 --> 00:12:17,854
and measure yourself against those goals,

379
00:12:17,854 --> 00:12:20,025
not against the driveway next door.

380
00:12:20,375 --> 00:12:21,431
Waste number nine.

381
00:12:21,611 --> 00:12:23,887
Over the top kids' parties and gear.

382
00:12:24,237 --> 00:12:26,581
Birthday parties with rented venues,

383
00:12:26,581 --> 00:12:28,405
entertainers and goody bags.

384
00:12:28,585 --> 00:12:31,812
Designer kids' clothes they'll outgrow in months.

385
00:12:31,992 --> 00:12:33,815
The newest gadgets for children.

386
00:12:33,995 --> 00:12:36,716
Many parents feel enormous pressure to give

387
00:12:36,716 --> 00:12:38,107
their kids everything,

388
00:12:38,107 --> 00:12:39,310
partly out of love,

389
00:12:39,310 --> 00:12:41,967
and partly because other parents are doing

390
00:12:41,967 --> 00:12:42,157
it.

391
00:12:42,507 --> 00:12:44,879
Kids rarely remember how much a party

392
00:12:44,879 --> 00:12:45,200
cost.

393
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:46,704
They remember time,

394
00:12:46,704 --> 00:12:47,958
attention and fun.

395
00:12:48,138 --> 00:12:50,604
And children who learn early that money

396
00:12:50,604 --> 00:12:52,881
is limited, and that choices matter,

397
00:12:52,881 --> 00:12:55,916
often grow into more financially capable adults.

398
00:12:56,096 --> 00:12:59,307
Stanley found that many millionaires deliberately avoided

399
00:12:59,307 --> 00:13:00,659
spoiling their children.

400
00:13:01,009 --> 00:13:01,926
The fix.

401
00:13:02,106 --> 00:13:04,207
Set a budget for parties and gifts

402
00:13:04,207 --> 00:13:05,751
before the season starts.

403
00:13:05,931 --> 00:13:08,102
Choose experiences over things.

404
00:13:08,282 --> 00:13:10,410
Buy kids' clothes and gear used when

405
00:13:10,410 --> 00:13:10,883
you can.

406
00:13:11,063 --> 00:13:13,440
And involve older kids in the choices,

407
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:15,755
which teaches them about money at the

408
00:13:15,755 --> 00:13:16,380
same time.

409
00:13:16,731 --> 00:13:17,822
Waste number ten.

410
00:13:18,002 --> 00:13:20,312
Vacations paid with credit cards.

411
00:13:20,663 --> 00:13:22,396
Vacations are wonderful,

412
00:13:22,396 --> 00:13:23,623
and rest matters.

413
00:13:23,803 --> 00:13:25,584
But putting a trip on a credit

414
00:13:25,584 --> 00:13:27,543
card you can't pay off right away

415
00:13:27,543 --> 00:13:29,562
means paying interest on memories.

416
00:13:29,742 --> 00:13:31,295
At twenty percent or more,

417
00:13:31,295 --> 00:13:33,685
a three thousand dollar vacation can end

418
00:13:33,685 --> 00:13:35,836
up costing hundreds of dollars more,

419
00:13:35,836 --> 00:13:37,808
and you could still be paying for

420
00:13:37,808 --> 00:13:38,705
it next summer.

421
00:13:39,055 --> 00:13:42,433
There's also research suggesting that people enjoy

422
00:13:42,433 --> 00:13:45,203
experiences more when they're paid for in

423
00:13:45,203 --> 00:13:47,770
advance, because the pain of paying is

424
00:13:47,770 --> 00:13:50,270
separated from the experience itself.

425
00:13:50,450 --> 00:13:52,413
Paying for a trip months later can

426
00:13:52,413 --> 00:13:53,971
take some of the joy out of

427
00:13:53,971 --> 00:13:54,606
the memory.

428
00:13:54,957 --> 00:13:55,816
The fix.

429
00:13:55,996 --> 00:13:58,713
Open a separate vacation savings account and

430
00:13:58,713 --> 00:14:00,628
automate a transfer each month.

431
00:14:00,808 --> 00:14:02,257
When the trip comes,

432
00:14:02,257 --> 00:14:02,910
pay cash.

433
00:14:03,090 --> 00:14:04,501
You'll enjoy it more,

434
00:14:04,501 --> 00:14:05,980
and it will cost less.

435
00:14:06,160 --> 00:14:08,318
And look for ways to travel cheaper,

436
00:14:08,318 --> 00:14:10,716
like off season dates or driving instead

437
00:14:10,716 --> 00:14:11,315
of flying.

438
00:14:11,666 --> 00:14:12,885
Waste number eleven.

439
00:14:13,065 --> 00:14:15,932
Renovations they expect to pay for themselves.

440
00:14:16,283 --> 00:14:19,064
Many homeowners believe a big remodel will

441
00:14:19,064 --> 00:14:20,985
add more value than it costs.

442
00:14:21,165 --> 00:14:23,671
But the annual Cost versus Value report,

443
00:14:23,671 --> 00:14:26,304
which compares the cost of common projects

444
00:14:26,304 --> 00:14:28,434
with the value they add at resale,

445
00:14:28,434 --> 00:14:31,380
has consistently found that most major remodels

446
00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:33,135
recoup less than their cost.

447
00:14:33,315 --> 00:14:36,777
Upscale kitchen and bathroom remodels often recover

448
00:14:36,777 --> 00:14:38,678
only part of what was spent.

449
00:14:39,029 --> 00:14:41,351
Renovating because you love your home and

450
00:14:41,351 --> 00:14:42,823
want to enjoy it is a fine

451
00:14:42,823 --> 00:14:43,220
choice.

452
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:45,500
The waste is renovating on debt and

453
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:47,661
telling yourself it's an investment.

454
00:14:47,841 --> 00:14:50,438
And sometimes the renovation is driven by

455
00:14:50,438 --> 00:14:51,896
what the neighbors did,

456
00:14:51,896 --> 00:14:53,797
not by what you actually need.

457
00:14:54,147 --> 00:14:55,017
The fix.

458
00:14:55,197 --> 00:14:56,538
Before a big project,

459
00:14:56,538 --> 00:14:58,901
look up the Cost versus Value numbers

460
00:14:58,901 --> 00:14:59,923
for your region.

461
00:15:00,103 --> 00:15:03,655
Prioritize repairs and maintenance over upgrades.

462
00:15:03,835 --> 00:15:05,533
And if you do renovate,

463
00:15:05,533 --> 00:15:06,861
save and pay cash,

464
00:15:06,861 --> 00:15:09,297
or at least know exactly what the

465
00:15:09,297 --> 00:15:11,068
loan will cost in total.

466
00:15:11,419 --> 00:15:12,440
Quick pause.

467
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:14,426
If you're finding this useful,

468
00:15:14,426 --> 00:15:16,231
subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

469
00:15:16,411 --> 00:15:18,087
It's free, it really helps,

470
00:15:18,087 --> 00:15:20,259
and we break down money topics like

471
00:15:20,259 --> 00:15:21,252
this every week.

472
00:15:21,432 --> 00:15:23,569
Okay, back to the psychology.

473
00:15:23,749 --> 00:15:26,487
Number twelve is growing faster than almost

474
00:15:26,487 --> 00:15:28,207
anything else on this list.

475
00:15:28,557 --> 00:15:29,927
Waste number twelve.

476
00:15:30,107 --> 00:15:31,012
Sports betting.

477
00:15:31,362 --> 00:15:34,024
Since a Supreme Court decision in twenty

478
00:15:34,024 --> 00:15:37,418
eighteen allowed states to legalize sports betting,

479
00:15:37,418 --> 00:15:39,548
it has spread across much of the

480
00:15:39,548 --> 00:15:40,081
country.

481
00:15:40,261 --> 00:15:43,245
The American Gaming Association has reported that

482
00:15:43,245 --> 00:15:46,108
Americans legally wagered well over one hundred

483
00:15:46,108 --> 00:15:48,362
billion dollars on sports in a single

484
00:15:48,362 --> 00:15:48,666
year.

485
00:15:48,846 --> 00:15:51,131
Betting apps make it possible to bet

486
00:15:51,131 --> 00:15:52,654
any time, from anywhere.

487
00:15:53,005 --> 00:15:56,022
Sportsbooks build an edge into their odds,

488
00:15:56,022 --> 00:15:56,956
so over time,

489
00:15:56,956 --> 00:15:58,752
the average bettor loses.

490
00:15:58,932 --> 00:16:01,611
Researchers have also found signs that easy

491
00:16:01,611 --> 00:16:03,916
access to betting is linked with more

492
00:16:03,916 --> 00:16:06,221
financial strain for some households,

493
00:16:06,221 --> 00:16:08,278
like lower savings and more debt.

494
00:16:08,458 --> 00:16:10,121
The apps are designed to keep you

495
00:16:10,121 --> 00:16:10,524
playing.

496
00:16:10,874 --> 00:16:11,837
The fix.

497
00:16:12,017 --> 00:16:13,218
If you bet for fun,

498
00:16:13,218 --> 00:16:15,365
set a strict monthly limit you can

499
00:16:15,365 --> 00:16:17,008
afford to lose completely,

500
00:16:17,008 --> 00:16:18,461
and never chase losses.

501
00:16:18,641 --> 00:16:20,638
Use the app's limit tools.

502
00:16:20,818 --> 00:16:23,097
And if betting is causing stress or

503
00:16:23,097 --> 00:16:24,661
debt, help is available,

504
00:16:24,661 --> 00:16:27,983
including the national problem gambling helpline at

505
00:16:27,983 --> 00:16:29,676
one eight hundred GAMBLER.

506
00:16:30,026 --> 00:16:31,303
Waste number thirteen.

507
00:16:31,483 --> 00:16:34,716
Upgrading phones and gadgets every year or

508
00:16:34,716 --> 00:16:35,024
two.

509
00:16:35,374 --> 00:16:38,330
Flagship phones can cost a thousand dollars

510
00:16:38,330 --> 00:16:38,880
or more.

511
00:16:39,060 --> 00:16:41,083
Many families have several,

512
00:16:41,083 --> 00:16:44,079
plus tablets, smart watches and laptops,

513
00:16:44,079 --> 00:16:47,001
often on payment plans bundled into the

514
00:16:47,001 --> 00:16:47,825
phone bill.

515
00:16:48,005 --> 00:16:50,490
The upgrade cycle is designed to make

516
00:16:50,490 --> 00:16:52,304
last year's model feel old,

517
00:16:52,304 --> 00:16:54,252
even when it works perfectly.

518
00:16:54,602 --> 00:16:57,150
Multiply that across a family of four,

519
00:16:57,150 --> 00:16:59,900
and gadget upgrades can cost thousands of

520
00:16:59,900 --> 00:17:00,906
dollars a year.

521
00:17:01,086 --> 00:17:04,470
Meanwhile, each new model usually brings small

522
00:17:04,470 --> 00:17:06,751
improvements over the last one.

523
00:17:06,931 --> 00:17:09,253
The excitement of new tech fades quickly.

524
00:17:09,433 --> 00:17:10,467
The payments don't.

525
00:17:10,817 --> 00:17:11,734
The fix.

526
00:17:11,914 --> 00:17:14,563
Keep your devices until they genuinely stop

527
00:17:14,563 --> 00:17:15,733
meeting your needs.

528
00:17:15,913 --> 00:17:18,605
Consider buying the previous year's model,

529
00:17:18,605 --> 00:17:20,592
or a certified refurbished one.

530
00:17:20,772 --> 00:17:22,568
And check your phone bill for device

531
00:17:22,568 --> 00:17:24,464
payments you may have forgotten about.

532
00:17:24,815 --> 00:17:26,312
Waste number fourteen.

533
00:17:26,492 --> 00:17:28,954
Brand names on everyday products.

534
00:17:29,304 --> 00:17:31,555
Groceries, cleaning products,

535
00:17:31,555 --> 00:17:33,030
medicine, clothing.

536
00:17:33,210 --> 00:17:36,148
Store brands often cost noticeably less.

537
00:17:36,328 --> 00:17:38,710
A study by economists published in the

538
00:17:38,710 --> 00:17:42,033
Quarterly Journal of Economics found that pharmacists

539
00:17:42,033 --> 00:17:44,415
and doctors were much more likely than

540
00:17:44,415 --> 00:17:47,048
other shoppers to buy store brand headache

541
00:17:47,048 --> 00:17:47,613
medicine.

542
00:17:47,793 --> 00:17:49,720
When people know what's in the box,

543
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:51,427
they stop paying for the brand.

544
00:17:51,777 --> 00:17:54,714
Brand loyalty is often built in childhood.

545
00:17:54,894 --> 00:17:56,566
We buy what our parents bought.

546
00:17:56,746 --> 00:17:59,310
And brands spend enormous sums on advertising

547
00:17:59,310 --> 00:18:00,449
to keep it that way.

548
00:18:00,629 --> 00:18:02,270
But for many everyday items,

549
00:18:02,270 --> 00:18:04,321
the difference is mostly packaging.

550
00:18:04,672 --> 00:18:05,624
The fix.

551
00:18:05,804 --> 00:18:08,044
Pick ten items you buy regularly and

552
00:18:08,044 --> 00:18:10,285
try the store brand version of each.

553
00:18:10,465 --> 00:18:12,555
Keep the swaps you can't tell apart.

554
00:18:12,735 --> 00:18:15,562
Many families save meaningful money every month

555
00:18:15,562 --> 00:18:18,029
this way, without feeling any difference.

556
00:18:18,379 --> 00:18:20,822
Why do these psychological traps hit the

557
00:18:20,822 --> 00:18:22,105
middle class so hard?

558
00:18:22,285 --> 00:18:23,876
Part of it is visibility.

559
00:18:24,056 --> 00:18:26,780
Lower income families often can't access the

560
00:18:26,780 --> 00:18:27,895
credit to keep up,

561
00:18:27,895 --> 00:18:30,372
and very wealthy families can afford to.

562
00:18:30,552 --> 00:18:32,375
The middle class is in between.

563
00:18:32,555 --> 00:18:34,323
Close enough to the next level to

564
00:18:34,323 --> 00:18:35,233
see it every day,

565
00:18:35,233 --> 00:18:37,001
and just able enough to borrow to

566
00:18:37,001 --> 00:18:37,698
reach for it.

567
00:18:38,048 --> 00:18:39,928
Another part is identity.

568
00:18:40,108 --> 00:18:42,701
Many middle class families feel that certain

569
00:18:42,701 --> 00:18:44,764
things define them as middle class.

570
00:18:44,944 --> 00:18:46,917
The house in the suburbs,

571
00:18:46,917 --> 00:18:49,127
two cars, a yearly vacation,

572
00:18:49,127 --> 00:18:50,626
kids in activities,

